\noindent\begin{minipage}{\textwidth}
\blueheading{Project Outcome / Deliverables (1 pts)}

\vspace{1mm}

\answerbox[5cm]{The final project will create a working Traffic Route Tracker that has a simple and easy to use interface. It will show a road network using a weighted graph and let people choose where they want to start and where they want to go. The system will. Show several possible routes at the same time each in different colors. Each route will show details, like how long it will take to travel how far it is, how busy the traffic is and how many traffic lights there are. The best choice will be shown as the route. Fake traffic situations will change how the routes are weighted and update the route when things change. Dijkstra’s algorithm will be used to find the fastest route and it will work with Data Structures like graphs, linked lists, queues and priority queues. The project will also have example situations that show how different traffic conditions affect which route is chosen. Overall the system will show how Data Structures, Algorithms, graph-based route planning and traffic simulation work together in a clear and easy to understand way.}
\end{minipage}

\vspace{8mm}

\noindent\begin{minipage}{\textwidth}
\blueheading{Assumptions}

\vspace{1mm}


\answerbox[5.9cm]{The Traffic Route Tracker will use a predefined road network instead of a full real map. Intersections or locations will be shown as graph vertices and roads will be shown as edges with predefined distance and base travel time. Traffic conditions will be simulated of taken from live data and will be labeled Low, Medium or High. These traffic conditions will change the estimated travel time of the roads.The system will show a few feasible routes at once to keep the interface clear. The recommended route will mainly be chosen by its estimated travel time and distance traffic level and the number of traffic signals will be shown for comparison. Traffic signals will come from map data.The system will not simulate vehicles or detailed real traffic flow. It will not need GPS, live traffic APIs or an internet connection, for its functions. Traffic changes will be added through predefined scenarios or user‑controlled inputs. The graphical interface will focus on showing routes and comparing them. The system will run on one computer using the chosen C/C++ environment and graphical framework.}
\end{minipage}

